<tr bgcolor=#F5DEB3><td colspan=2 align=center valign=middle><b>Only The Fittest Survive</b></td></tr>
<tr bgcolor=#F5DEB3><td colspan=2 align=left valign=middle>I was hatched in the nurseries of the Fortress Talishan, in the ruined city of Cabilis.  I have only vague memories of this time, of a steamy warm room with dry sandy floors, a pool of blood-warm water to swim in, and plentiful food.  I remember my broodmates, and how we would fight each other, snapping and snarling and lashing our tails.  I remember the taste of warm blood and fresh meat.
<br><Br>
The infancy of an Iksar is harsh, and only the fittest survive.
<br><Br><hr>
Came a day that our childhood sanctuary was invaded, by a great tall adult male, his scaled hide seamed with scars.  I can still see in my mind's eye how he stood there, alone, and taunted us.  The foolhardy charged him, and died quickly.  The frightened backed away, thus reducing themselves to a life of servitude.  Those of us who remained, who neither charged nor shrank away but stood our grounds, we were snatched up in turn, examined for deformities, and then removed from that room.  There were but three of us, and after the examination, only two - a brood-sister died that day, mercifully put out of the agony her ill-healed legs had caused her since one of our earliest fights.
<br><Br>
The childhood of an Iksar is harsh, and only the fittest survive.
<br><Br><hr>
My brood-brother and I were taken from the hatchling chamber, and put in a stone-walled, stone-floored room, a stone trough of cold, turbid water our only amenity.  No more the steamy warmth we had enjoyed, the soft sands, the tussling fights.  Each day, an aged Iksar entered, and would set us a task.  We knew him only as "Master".
<br><Br>
For hours, we would stand and lash our tails against the walls, or kick a large ball around the room, or spar with eat other, snapping and snarling in earnest as the elderly Iksar goaded us on.  Some times he gave us sticks to wield, and then we would beat at each other, trying to see which of us could give the other the worst bruises.
<br><Br>
Afterwards, once he had left again, we would comfort each other as best we could, soaking bruises and cleaning the bitemarks and scrapes on each others' hides.
<br><Br>
Ah, how we dreamed of being warriors, and fighting at each other's side.  Long and many were the tales we spun for each other of what our future adventures would be. From things our Master had said, we had heard of the ancient enemies of our peoples, the goblins and sarnaks and giants.  And we had also heard of the strange soft-hided peoplea who had come to our shores of late.  We dreamed of winning honor killing our foes, and of some day travelling to see the lands where the strange people had come from, but it was not to be.
<br><Br>
My brood-brother slipped one day, emerging from the trough, and his head hit the edge with a sickening crunch.  A sad, stupid death for one who had been fated to be a warrior.  Master entered the next day for our lesson, and found me crouched by the lifeless body of my brood-brother, still keening in grief.  For a moment his own control slipped and he, too, keened softly.  Patting me on the shoulder, he bade me go sleep, and removed the stiffened husk that had been my dearest friend.
<br><Br>
The days were long and empty after that.  Where before I had filled my spare time daydreaming and talking, now I found that only the mind-numbing routine of vigorous excercise could prevent grief and loneliness from overwhelming me.  I would labor until I sank into exhausted sleep, then labor again as soon as I had awoken, eaten, and slaked my thirst.
<br><Br>
Eventually the pain passed, and only the loneliness remained.  Master was pleased with my progress, my vigorous excercising having given me a strength and control rarely seen in female Iksar.  Came a day he told me I was ready to move on to the next step of my training.  Giving me a note to take to a senior trainer, he released me from my solitude, and I walked the corridors of the Fortress for the first time on my own.
<br><Br>
The adolescense of an Iksar is harsh, and only the fittest survive.</td></tr>
